 "public_summary": "There is a consistent, flat-bottomed ~640 ppm dip every 2.48 days on the M-dwarf, seen in 41 transits with no odd/even or secondary eclipses and a centroid on the target. The depth implies a ~2 Earth-radius planet, and no neighbour within one TESS pixel can plausibly produce it. The duration is shorter than central but fine for a low-impact transit; the modest overall scatter is consistent with the star's own spot variability.",
 "observing": {
  "headline": "Tessa found a 2 Earth-radius candidate with 2.48013-day period and 643 ppm depth lasting 1.4 h around K dwarf TIC 166321785 (Tmag 11.1, 4383 K, 96 pc away).",
  "next_chances": [
   "SAAO, South Africa: 2027-01-15 23:42 to 2027-01-16 02:09 UTC (+/-2.4 min)",
   "Cerro Tololo, Chile: 2027-02-12 06:24 to 2027-02-12 08:51 UTC (+/-2.5 min)",
   "Cerro Tololo, Chile: 2027-02-17 05:26 to 2027-02-17 07:53 UTC (+/-2.5 min)",
   "Cerro Tololo, Chile: 2027-02-22 04:28 to 2027-02-22 06:55 UTC (+/-2.5 min)"
  ],
  "telescope": "Per-transit SNR is 0.9 on a 0.4 m, 2.1 on a 1 m and 3.6 on a 2 m, so no telescope can clearly catch one transit alone; a 2 m needs a few transits stacked, while 0.4 m and 1 m would need many transits stacked or a space telescope.",
  "refuted_if": "It is refuted if high-quality observations covering the predicted windows from 2027-01-15 23:42 to 2027-01-16 02:09 UTC and from 2027-02-12 06:24 to 2027-02-12 08:51 UTC show no 643 ppm dip within 2.4 to 2.5 min of prediction, or if the dip depth changes with colour, indicating a blended binary.",
  "confirmed_by": "It would be confirmed as a planet if repeated transits with 643 ppm depth and 1.4 h duration recur on the 2.48013-day period within 2.3 min and radial velocities give a planetary mass.",
